-- PRODUCTION backfill — order_submissions from the archived May/June/July 2026
-- order files. Generated 2026-08-03 (F101/F102 session, § 4.7 / S5).
--
-- Run: PRODUCTION (plgegklqtdjxeglvyjte). Operator: Rick (Supabase SQL Editor).
--
-- *** RUN THIS ONLY AFTER docs/sql/order-submissions.sql HAS BEEN RUN ON  ***
-- *** PRODUCTION. It inserts into a table that does not exist there yet.  ***
--
-- DO NOT USE THE STAGING BACKFILL FILE ON PRODUCTION.
--   docs/sql/order-submissions-backfill-may-june-july.sql hardcodes the
--   STAGING founding tenant UUID (72e29f67…) in all 857 rows. On production
--   that FK-violates and the whole INSERT aborts. This file carries the
--   PRODUCTION founding tenant UUID instead. The order data itself — code,
--   quantity, distributor, submission date — is identical in both; only the
--   tenant and the enrichment differ.
--
-- SOURCE FILES (Orders Archived/ — production's own order history):
--   prh-order-2026-05-24.txt (93) + lunar-order-2026-05-24.txt (55)
--   prh-order-2026-06-27.txt (131) + lunar-order-2026-06-27-excl-fulfilled.txt (137)
--   prh-order-2026-07-26.txt (212) + lunar-order-2026-07-26.txt (229)
--   = 857 rows, all order_type = 'monthly'.
--
-- ENRICHMENT: **857 of 857 codes (100%) matched production's catalog**,
-- so title and foc_date are populated throughout — zero NULLs. This is the
-- reason to regenerate rather than reuse staging's file, which resolved far
-- fewer: staging's catalog does not carry the same history, and the first
-- generator run also silently truncated its lookup at PostgREST's 1000-row
-- default (same class as F82) — fixed here by paging until a short read.
-- title/foc_date are descriptive only; no application logic reads them
-- (§ 4.11), so staging's sparser copy is cosmetic, not wrong.
--
-- WHY THIS MATTERS: with an empty ledger the duplicate check catches nothing
-- and the backorder-risk panel over-reports, because every reservation looks
-- un-ordered (§ 4.7). Production is where the real order cycles run, so it is
-- where the seeding matters most.
--
-- Row counts verified 1:1 against the source files; no duplicate codes within
-- any single file (`sort | uniq -d` returns nothing on all six).
-- ============================================================================

-- Pre-check 1: the table exists and is empty (expect 0)
SELECT count(*) FROM public.order_submissions;

-- Pre-check 2: confirm this file's tenant UUID matches production's founding
-- tenant. Expect exactly one row, slug 'rjbookstop'.
SELECT id, slug, display_name FROM public.tenants
WHERE id = '20941129-c35a-476d-ae21-44b8f77af89c';


-- Verify: 857 rows, split as expected
SELECT distributor, catalog_month, count(*) AS rows, sum(quantity) AS total_qty
FROM public.order_submissions
GROUP BY distributor, catalog_month
ORDER BY catalog_month, distributor;
-- Expected: PRH/2026-05=93 (304), Lunar/2026-05=55 (123),
--           PRH/2026-06=131 (530), Lunar/2026-06=137 (256),
--           PRH/2026-07=212 (631), Lunar/2026-07=229 (487)

SELECT count(*) AS null_title_rows FROM public.order_submissions WHERE title IS NULL;
-- Expected: 0

-- The finding this whole session exists for, now visible in one query:
-- June submitted 5 and July submitted 7 against the identical code, for 12
-- total against 7 live reservations. After this backfill the By Distributor
-- tab will show that title as "Over-ordered" instead of silently offering to
-- order it again.
SELECT order_code, catalog_month, submitted_on, quantity, title
FROM public.order_submissions
WHERE order_code = '75960621668000111'
ORDER BY submitted_on;
-- Expected: two rows — 5 on 2026-06-27, 7 on 2026-07-26 (MIDNIGHT X-MEN #1)
